WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:09.865
This is the Week 9 fingerboard deck, built in Onshape from a blank Part Studio. Pause after each step and do the same in your own document.

00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:17.775
First, set the workspace units to millimeters. Your calipers read in mm, and so does the printer.

00:00:18.325 --> 00:00:26.290
Now the variables. #bolt is the bolt diameter you measured with calipers. Ours is 2 mm. Type your own number.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:35.431
#clear is your clearance: the deviation from your Week 5 calibration cube. Ours is 0.3 mm.

00:00:35.981 --> 00:00:44.921
#hole is a formula: #bolt + #clear = 2.3 mm. If you re-measure, change one number and every hole updates.

00:00:45.471 --> 00:00:50.010
Start a sketch on the Top plane. Draw a center-point rectangle on the origin.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.591
Dimension it: 96 mm long,

00:00:54.141 --> 00:00:58.402
and 32 mm wide. The lines turn black: fully defined.

00:00:58.952 --> 00:01:03.654
Round the corners with the sketch fillet tool: 5 mm on each corner.

00:01:04.204 --> 00:01:08.708
Extrude the outline 2.5 mm. That's the deck thickness.

00:01:09.258 --> 00:01:11.882
Here's the blank deck. Now the truck holes.

00:01:12.432 --> 00:01:15.985
Pick the top face of the deck and start a new sketch on it.

00:01:16.535 --> 00:01:20.970
Draw one circle. For its diameter, type #hole, not a number.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:29.867
Place it from the origin. We used 28 mm along and 3 mm across. Use the hole spacing you measured on your trucks.

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:34.609
Extrude the circle with Remove and Through all. That cuts the first hole.

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:46.385
A linear pattern copies the hole. Feature pattern: 8 mm along, then 6 mm across. Four holes, one truck.

00:01:46.935 --> 00:01:52.183
Mirror the hole and the pattern across the Right plane. Now the second truck has its holes too.

00:01:52.733 --> 00:02:02.462
Here's the deck: 96 × 32 × 2.5 mm, 8 holes. It sits flat on the plate, so it prints with no supports.

00:02:03.012 --> 00:02:13.937
To print it, right-click the part and choose Export. Pick 3MF, name it clearly, and export. If you use STL, set units to millimeters first.
